            <ArticleTitle>Characteristics of patients who attribute chronic pain to minor injury.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>27-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Eighty-four chronic low back pain (CLBP) patients were studied in order to determine whether personality differences existed between patients reporting a minor injury at work as the cause of their disability and ensuing insurance claims, and patients who did not. Twenty-three of the CLBP patients studied reported that their pain resulted from a accident at work. All the subjects underwent a variety of psychological and somatic tests. The results indicate that the &quot;accident&quot; patient group is characterized by specific psycho-dynamic personality patterns significantly different from the characteristics of the &quot;non-accident&quot; group. The characteristics of the patients in the &quot;accident&quot; group suggest that these patients tend to hold on to minor injuries, and that one possible reason for this might partly be related to the liberal insurance policies in Sweden.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
